## People behind Allmaps

Allmaps is shaped by people with backgrounds in cartography, software
development, design and cultural heritage.

- **Bert Spaan** and **Jules Schoonman** — Founded Allmaps, lead its development
  and manage the project.
- [Manuel Claeys Bouuaert](https://manuelclaeysbouuaert.be/) —  Freelance
  facilitator and cartographer. Developer and strategic thinker for Allmaps,
  expert in the mathematics that make it all work.
- [Ian Spangler](https://itspangler.com/) — Was
  Assistant Curator of Digital & Participatory Geography at the Leventhal Map
  & Education Center, where he directed the NEH-funded project that supported
  Allmaps’ development.
- [Luuk van de Ven](https://luukvandeven.nl/) — Brand designer and art director.
  Develops Allmaps’ visual identity and interface designs.
- [Bryan Haberberger](https://habesoftware.rocks/) — Developer and independent
  consultant who played a key role in writing the
  [IIIF Georeference Extension specification](https://iiif.io/api/extension/georef/).

Many others contribute code, documentation, ideas and feedback through
[GitHub](https://github.com/allmaps/), the
[IIIF Maps Community Group](https://iiif.io/community/groups/maps/) and our
[institutional partners](/partners).

## Project principles

### Open data in, open data out

Allmaps works with maps published through IIIF and brings georeferencing from
across collections together as [open data](/data). The images stay with the
institutions that publish them. We make it easy for institutions to bring the
georeference data back into their own systems, so work done with Allmaps can
improve access to maps wherever their collections are used.

### Data should outlive the software

The work people put into georeferencing should remain useful beyond Allmaps.
We use open, documented standards such as
[Georeference Annotations](https://iiif.io/api/extension/georef/), so people
can download, preserve, adapt and reuse the data in other projects. Access to
that data should never depend on a subscription or continued use of our tools.

### Small tools that work together

Allmaps is an ecosystem of [open source libraries, plugins and applications](/open-source).
These tools can be used together or combined with other software. We keep
individual tools focused and simple, so people can use the parts they need
and build on them for their own projects.

### Easy to start, open to everyone

Working with digitized maps should be approachable and fun. You can start
georeferencing with Allmaps Editor without creating an account or setting up
specialist software. We aim to keep the tools easy to use and collect as
little personal data as possible.

## How Allmaps is organized

Bert Spaan and Jules Schoonman lead Allmaps’ development and manage the
project. They work with independent developers and institutional partners to
turn shared needs into open source tools that benefit the wider community.

The Allmaps Advisory Board helps steer the project and sets priorities for
the annual roadmap together with the Allmaps team. It brings together
representatives from Allmaps Innovators, the IIIF Consortium and long-term
institutional partners, alongside the founders.

Through the [IIIF-Allmaps partnership](/iiif-partnership), institutions support
ongoing maintenance and new development. Partners receive dedicated services,
training and support, while Allmaps’ software and georeference data remain
open.

Interested in working with Allmaps? [Get in touch](/contact) or learn how your
institution can [support the project](/partners).
